Day 7 — PrintNightmare (CVE-2021-1675 / CVE-2021-34527) → SYSTEM
Abuse the Windows Print Spooler's remote driver-install RPC (`RpcAddPrinterDriverEx`)
to make the target load an attacker-hosted DLL as SYSTEM, catch a reverse shell, and
read the Administrator flag.

Item	Value
Target	10.10.0.113 (Windows Server 2016, Spooler enabled)
Attacker (Kali)	100.100.4.255 (`tun0`)
Login	User / 123456
Result	nt authority\system
🚩 Flag	cybered{Pr1ntN1ghtm4r3_Expl01t3d}
Why it works
The Print Spooler (`spoolsv.exe`) runs as SYSTEM and, over MS-RPRN/MS-PAR, lets
clients install printer drivers remotely. PrintNightmare is a broken privilege check:
any authenticated user can point RpcAddPrinterDriverEx at \attacker\share\evil.dll
and the SYSTEM spooler downloads and loads it. Spooler is on by default → low-priv user
to SYSTEM in one hop.

